#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153084} :activity/id #uuid "69f74c8a-9d6c-5d27-b2eb-12f32044cc0f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033 :topic/id #uuid "27991def-afa0-5245-84f6-cb161dd1a2bc" :topic/math-academy-id 2707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153362 :tutorial/content "A **local extremum** (or **relative extremum**) of a function is a point that is either a maximum or minimum point of the function relative to an (open) interval in the function's domain. \n\nFor example, consider the function shown in the graph below. \n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe graph has a peak at the point $A$ where $x=0.$ We say that the function has a **local maximum** when $x=0.$ We also say that the point $A$ is a **local maximum point** of the function. \n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=0$ such that $A$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph has a valley at the point $B$ where $x=2.$ We say that the function has a **local minimum** when $x=2.$ We can also say that the point $B$ is a **local minimum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=2$ such that $B$ will be the lowest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "63ce69cb-9024-5fca-baae-f6f89fe530a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4078 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85da6730-a1ac-55ee-945d-f7a140cd4455" :step/math-academy-id 24347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151973 :question/id #uuid "986cc1f3-747e-53cb-845d-411a21c9bc23" :question/math-academy-id "e-6683"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e049969f-d389-5d67-a96c-4e0430942925" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548991}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Function Visually"} :step/id #uuid "2870450e-ff69-57b9-a345-eb615a023b41" :step/math-academy-id 24348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152216 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152026 :question/id #uuid "fa76f558-adeb-5d8d-9273-70f2d8a76f97" :question/math-academy-id "e-7970"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05fd062c-7474-5f4d-aa40-06913fb420df" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549062}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Function Using a Condition"} :step/id #uuid "1237e192-4ede-579f-907b-fa8dee77c024" :step/math-academy-id 29746 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152217 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152027 :question/id #uuid "2cfd4563-e033-50c6-8e39-55295e81b4da" :question/math-academy-id "e-7971"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9296cdb-31d8-5855-b605-a47c33b21c85" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549035}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding a Function's Local Extrema"} :step/id #uuid "c6ea2a5d-7d63-54f9-930e-d0df5623dea2" :step/math-academy-id 29747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152028 :question/id #uuid "10bb268e-60a2-5e88-9c78-f26dc11db1a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7972"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c98ce69-8410-51a4-8d9c-eec7b46fb83b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549068}] :knowledge-point/title "Global vs. Local Extrema"} :step/id #uuid "5e9dba89-e765-5561-bf39-1a13f654a602" :step/math-academy-id 29748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153406 :tutorial/content "Can a point on a graph be both a local minimum *and* a local maximum?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the function $f(x)$ given by the graph below. \n\n![](images/0c/0cec91d9ec8e108ee4836e816aae63722fc4c09d997790eaf283cd3e2a9c4bc6.png)\n\nNow consider the point where $x=2.$ Our goal is to determine which type of extremum it is. To do this, we consider the values of $f(x)$ in the open interval $(1.5, 2.5),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6cf1a6237c50d5c2b651937ca46b9ae52a08dafb831d76b27ddb068d37dd501.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local maximum because $f(x) \\le f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local minimum because $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is both a local maximum *and* a local minimum of $f(x)!$ \n\nThis might seem strange. But, notice that $x=2$ lies within an interval where the function is constant. This is a special case where a particular point is both a local minimum and a local maximum." :tutorial/id #uuid "5e6aefda-fcc2-5ef4-88df-41bc5e862aac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4806 :tutorial/title "Special Cases of Extrema"} :step/id #uuid "2a3a9ac9-b320-5641-ba01-883737be2301" :step/math-academy-id 29849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153407 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to looking at the function $y=f(x)$ that we saw earlier.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe point $A$ where $x=0$ is a local maximum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=0$ such that the point $A$ is the highest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (-0.5,0.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *less than or equal to* $f(0),$ the largest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\leq f(0)$ for all $x\\in(-0.5, 0.5).$\n\nSimilarly, the point $B$ where $x=2$ is a local minimum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=2$ such that the point $B$ is the lowest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (1.5,2.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *greater than or equal to* $f(2),$ the smallest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\geq f(2)$ for all $x\\in(1.5, 2.5).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d3d44d79-c497-5b0e-a272-62a35d8b9338" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4836 :tutorial/title "Writing a Condition for a Point To Be a Local Extremum"} :step/id #uuid "994e317d-e873-5b6a-b054-ffe802b777c5" :step/math-academy-id 30168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153156}}] :activity/title "Local Extrema of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152350}]}
